**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) platform supporting multi-user operations with role hierarchy, temporary delegation, clearance-based authorization, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces security constraints such as clearance-based permissions, role delegation and revocation, and preserves safety, liveness, and integrity under concurrent operations. A new Emergency Module is to be integrated, enabling a global/system-wide operational mode and privileges during emergencies, with specific triggering, duration, and exit requirements. Emergency mode is a system-wide state: when Emergency mode is triggered, all active sessions enter Emergency mode together, and no session may remain in or independently return to Normal mode until the Emergency ends. Only one Emergency may be active at a time. Administrators may not initiate or participate in separate concurrent Emergencies or acquire independent emergency privileges through different sessions. Emergency privileges and policy relaxations apply consistently across all sessions and are revoked globally when the system returns to Normal mode. Emergency mode can be triggered exactly once per session by exactly 2 administrators, is active for an abstract fixed duration but may be ended early by the same 2 administrators, and grants special privileges only to the triggering administrators, applied globally across all sessions. While Emergency mode is active, new triggers are blocked. Roles delegated during Emergency mode are revoked upon exit (with timing as specified in functional requirements), while roles delegated prior persist as per their policy.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Every execution must contain exactly one Emergency trigger, causing the system to enter Emergency mode.
- Only exactly 2 administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode when the system is in Normal mode; each session may only trigger Emergency mode once. Emergency mode entry is permitted only under these conditions.
- Emergency mode is a global/system-wide state and is time-limited by an abstract fixed duration and cannot persist indefinitely; the system must ensure eventual return to Normal mode for all sessions together. Early termination is permitted by the same 2 administrators who triggered Emergency mode.
- Every triggered emergency must have a definite end time, set by either timeout or early termination. The system MUST return to NormalMode for all sessions together no later than the end time of the emergency, and restoration must not be delayed past this time.
- Emergency mode, once triggered, must persist for at least one time step before transition back to Normal mode is allowed.
- No new Emergency mode triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.
- During Emergency mode, request processing order may be bypassed to prioritize high classification requests; this relaxation is temporary and applies system-wide.
- Users may temporarily hold predefined mutually exclusive roles only during Emergency mode. Mutual exclusivity must be symmetric (if A is mutex with B, then B is mutex with A) and paired, and roles cannot be mutex with themselves.
- In Emergency mode, if a user holds two mutually exclusive roles, at least one must have been delegated.
- Emergency privileges are always empty in Normal mode. Only the two administrators who triggered Emergency mode may hold emergency privileges, and only during Emergency mode. At all other times and for all other users, emergency privileges must not be assigned.
- All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked at the Emergency end, with revocation to occur before or at the first time step of Normal mode. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode persist until expiry or administrator revocation.
- Administrators who held emergency privileges are required to update their credentials immediately upon exit from Emergency mode before resuming normal operations.
- Self-delegation is prohibited: a user cannot delegate a role to themselves.
- Only directly assigned roles may be delegated; delegation of delegated roles is prohibited.
- Delegation authority is restricted to users with direct assignment of the role.
- The expiry of a delegated role must be strictly after its delegation time.
- For delegation initiation, only the time of delegation is to be used to mark when a delegation is initiated. Whether a delegation is an Emergency-mode delegation is determined by the system mode at the time of delegation initiation.
- For every request, the request processor (the entity processing the request) must be recorded, regardless of whether the request was approved or rejected.
- All administrators have high clearance, but not all high-clearance users are administrators.
- The system must record the time at which a delegated role is revoked early and the admin responsible for the revocation.
- Emergency-mode delegations are permitted but not required; the absence of such delegations in a given execution is an acceptable system behavior for verification purposes.
- Each user must be assigned at least one role consistent with their clearance level. Delegations can only be made from a user to another user whose clearance is lower than or equal to that of the delegator; delegations to users with higher clearance are forbidden.
- The requirements shall specify (or the model shall empirically determine) the minimum number of users and clearance levels necessary such that delegation is feasible in every execution, given the above assignment and delegation constraints.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
Every execution must contain exactly one Emergency trigger, which is jointly initiated by exactly 2 administrators while the system is in Normal mode. This causes the system to enter Emergency mode globally. Each session may only trigger Emergency mode once, and no new emergency triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
For every Emergency trigger, the system MUST return to NormalMode for all sessions together no later than the emergency's end time, and remain in NormalMode for all subsequent times. Emergency mode may end either by an abstract fixed duration (timeout) or by early termination by the same 2 administrators who triggered it. The return to NormalMode must occur at or before the end time; restoration must not be delayed past this time. Emergency mode, once triggered, must persist for at least one time step before transition back to Normal mode is allowed.

R3: Modified Processing and Privileges During Emergency  
R3.1: During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize multiple high classification requests simultaneously, system-wide.  
R3.2: During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles (as defined in the set of mutually exclusive abstract roles). If a user holds two mutually exclusive roles during Emergency mode, at least one must have been delegated (mutex role bypass requirement).  
R3.3: Emergency privileges are always empty in Normal mode. Only the two administrators who triggered Emergency mode may hold emergency privileges, and only during Emergency mode. At all other times and for all other users, emergency privileges must not be assigned.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1: Emergency privileges must be revoked globally for all triggering administrators at the end of Emergency mode, prior to the first time step of Normal mode; at no time in NormalMode may any administrator hold emergency privileges.  
R4.2: All delegations that are initiated during Emergency mode must be revoked before or at the first time step of Normal mode. Delegations initiated in Normal mode, even if active during Emergency, do not require revocation at this transition. Delegations made during Normal mode, before Emergency mode, can persist across Emergency mode, unless revoked by an admin or expired during Emergency. Delegations during Normal mode must continue until their expiry or revocation, regardless of the system's mode transition. If a delegation is revoked before expiration, the identity of the administrator performing the revocation must be recorded.  
R4.3: Administrators who held emergency privileges must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends, before resuming normal operations.

R5: Delegation Requirements  
R5.1: A user cannot delegate a role to themselves (self-delegation prohibition).  
R5.2: Only directly assigned roles may be delegated; delegation of delegated roles is prohibited.  
R5.3: Delegation authority is restricted to users with direct assignment of the role.  
R5.4: The expiry of a delegated role must be strictly after its delegation time.  
R5.5: Each user must be assigned at least one role consistent with their clearance level.  
R5.6: Delegations can only be made to users whose clearance is lower than or equal to that of the delegator; delegations to users with higher clearance are forbidden.

R6: Mutual Exclusion (Mutex) Role Requirements  
R6.1: Mutually exclusive roles must be defined in pairs and mutual exclusivity must be symmetric: if Role A is mutex with Role B, then Role B is mutex with Role A.  
R6.2: A role cannot be mutually exclusive with itself.

R7: Request Processing Logging  
R7.1: For every request (regardless of outcome), the system must record the processor responsible for the request.

R8: Delegation Coverage Verification  
R8.1: For verification purposes, the model must demonstrate that there is at least one execution where a delegation is initiated and active during Emergency mode, and at least one execution where a delegation is initiated and active during Normal mode.  
R8.2: The model must guarantee that every execution contains exactly one Emergency trigger causing entry into Emergency mode, and that each user is assigned at least one role.  
R8.3: The requirements shall specify or the model shall empirically determine the minimum number of users and clearance levels necessary such that delegation is feasible in every execution, given the assignment and delegation constraints.
```

### ABSTRACTION GUIDANCE:
Ask questions only when the answer affects what must be verified, not how the requirement is implemented or modeled.

Ask when:
- Behavioral ambiguity affects verification
- Cardinality affects consistency
- Temporal ordering matters
- Missing scenarios need confirmation
- A requirement may be ambiguous, inconsistent, incomplete, or logically weak.


Do not ask when:
- the issue is only an implementation or modeling choice,
- the detail can be safely abstracted,
- different answers would not change the verification structure.

### DEFAULT ASSUMPTION POLICY
If the user does not answer a clarification question, continue with the smallest reasonable assumption.

Use the weakest assumption that:
- is enough to proceed,
- preserves the abstract requirement intent,
- and adds no unnecessary constraints or behavior.

State the assumption explicitly and treat it as provisional.

### RESPONSE FORMAT FOR INTERPRETATION:
Return exactly the sections listed below and nothing else. Do not add any extra text outside this format.
```
=== RESULT INTERPRETATION ===
[For EACH issue/change found, include:]
- What: [Brief description of the issue/result]
- Expected vs. Actual: [Was this expected per regression log? If not, what was expected?]
- Likely Cause: [model issue / requirement issue / missing assumption / scope limitation]

=== REGRESSION DIAGNOSIS ===
Discrepancies: [List any unexpected changes compared to expected impact in regression log, or "None - changes match expected impact"]
Analysis: [Why discrepancies occured?]

=== OUTCOME CLASSIFICATION ===
Classification: [expected_improvement / unintended_regression / spec_clarification: <rationale>]
Rationale: [1 sentence explanation]


=== BLOCKING QUESTIONS ===
[Include clarification questions from recommended_follow_up in UNSAT Matrix (if exists). Format as numbered questions that address the recommended clarifications.]
[For each issue classified as requirement issue or assumption requiring confirmation, include:]
1. [specific question for the user]
  Why needed: [what ambiguity, missing condition, conflict, or assumption it resolves]
2. [specific question for the user]
  Why needed:
OR
- No question needed: [brief reason why]
```

### CONVERGENCE CRITERIA:

Complete when ALL conditions are met:

**HARD METRICS (must all pass):**
1. No syntax errors
2. No counterexamples
3. All positive run commands satisfied (excluding "negative" test cases)

**AGENT ASSESSMENT (evaluated when hard metrics pass):**
4. Satisfying instances are meaningful and comprehensive
5. No vacuity or underspecification detected
6. All requirements adequately verified
7. User indicates satisfaction or provides no additional feedback

Convergence = Hard Metrics Pass AND Agent Assessment Recommends TRUE

### UNSATISFIABLE PREDICATES ASSESSMENT:

Purpose: Analyze UNSAT predicates as possible evidence of a modeling error, overconstraint, missing assumption, insufficient scope, requirement conflict, requirement gap, or an overly strong witness scenario.

### CRITICAL RULE: Persistent Unsatisfiability Detection
If the same predicate remains UNSAT for 2+ consecutive iterations, treat it as an unresolved blocker.
Do NOT recommend convergence, “no change,” “keep fix,” or “stop improving” unless there is evidence that the predicate is intentionally impossible under the requirements.

### Systematic Unsatisfiability Analysis (MANDATORY for UNSAT predicate):

1. Decompose the predicate into key atomic constraints.
   Identify state, transition, existence, ordering, classification, relationship, guard constraints, and preconditions

2. Identify the likely blocking points:
Ask which constraint or combination of constraints prevents satisfiability:
   - Temporal conflicts: Can required events occur in the specified order?
   - Resource conflicts: Are sufficient resources available with required properties?
   - Logical contradictions: Do any constraints require mutually exclusive states?
   - Ordering paradoxes: Do sequencing rules create circular dependencies?
   - Scope limitations: Is the search space artificially constrained?

3. Map the likely blocker to requirements.
   Explain whether the UNSAT result suggests a requirement conflict, ambiguity/gap, modeling error, overconstraint, insufficient scope, or overly strong scenario predicate.

4. Propose concrete diagnostic experiments.
   Give specific smaller `run` predicates or relaxed variants that isolate the suspected blocker.

5. Propose specific diagnostic tests:
   - Test 1: Relax the most restrictive constraint
   - Test 2: Separate combined constraints to test independently
   - Test 3: Increase scope or bounds if applicable
   - Test 4: Remove temporal ordering to test state reachability
   - Test 5: Simplify to minimal scenario that still exhibits the issue

   If diagnostic predicates were generated in a previous iteration, identify the first transition from SAT to UNSAT, classify the likely root cause, and choose the next action based on evidence rather than repeating the prior UNSAT feedback.

6. Requirement Clarification Questions:
   - Ask specific questions about potentially conflicting requirements and priority when requirements conflict in the ``BLOCKING QUESTIONS`` section
   - Determine if exceptions or special cases should apply

## Requirement-Witness Rule
If the UNSAT predicate is intended to witness a requirement, verify that the witness does not encode more than the requirement states.


## For UNSAT baseline predicates:**
- Contradiction in baseline requirements → Stop all further analysis
- Modeling error → Provide exact location and fix
- Overconstraint → Identify which constraint to relax
- Insufficient scope → Suggest specific scope increase

## For combined requirements (R1, R1R2, R1R2R3...):**
- Use incremental analysis to find breaking point
- When R1R2 is SAT but R1R2R3 is UNSAT:
  * R3 introduces the conflict
  * Identify which specific aspect of R3 conflicts with R1/R2
  * Suggest minimal modification to R3

### UNSAT Matrix Construction
For each relevant requirement from the requirement document, or fact, assertion, assumption, or scope bound from the Alloy model, classify its relationship to the UNSAT predicate as one of:
•	supports_scenario: helps make the UNSAT scenario possible or required.
•	blocks_scenario: prevents or restricts the UNSAT scenario.
•	irrelevant: does not affect the UNSAT scenario.
•	unknown: may affect the scenario, but more diagnostic evidence is needed.

Analysis Rules
Do not accept an UNSAT predicate as expected behavior. If a requirement-witness predicate remains UNSAT, identify what blocks the witness or explain why the witness is stronger than the requirement.
If a source is marked blocks_scenario, explain whether it is:
•	a valid requirement constraint,
•	an accidental overconstraint,
•	a modeling error,
•	a missing exception,
•	or evidence of requirement inconsistency.
If a source is marked unknown, propose a concrete diagnostic predicate or experiment to determine whether it supports or blocks the scenario.

